    description: "run_survey's `max_files` is documented as \"Optional maximum files per language\nanalyzer. Limits how many files each analyzer processes (for large repos).\"\nMeasured on this repo it binds on some analyzers and is ignored by others.\n\nAt max_files=1, files_analyzed per pass:\n\n    python        1  of 1121   capped\n    javascript    1  of   14   capped\n    bash         43  of   43   IGNORED\n    markdown    132  of  132   IGNORED\n    json         34  of   34   IGNORED\n    toml         10  of   10   IGNORED\n    go/rust/swift/java/gitignore/requirements ...  IGNORED\n\nTotal: 252 of 1385 files still analyzed with the cap set to 1.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS. The flag exists as an escape hatch for large repos, and a user\nwho sets it to bound a run gets a partial bound with no indication which\nanalyzers honored it. It is also a measurement hazard: I used the cap to sweep\nper-file cost and the non-binding analyzers put a floor under every data point,\nwhich produced a nonsensical NEGATIVE intercept (-12.3s) from the linear fit.\nThe sweep is still usable as an upper bound on fixed cost, but only because the\nsmallest configuration is a real measured configuration rather than an\nextrapolation.\n\nFIX SHAPE: either honor the cap at the shared find_files/discovery chokepoint so\nit applies uniformly by construction, or narrow the documented promise to name\nwhich analyzers it governs. The chokepoint version is preferable — it is one\nplace, and \"each analyzer remembers to apply the cap\" is the kind of per-site\nobligation that is already only 2-for-21 compliant."  # 3f98
    fields: {}  # 3f98
